      "textEn": "6\n\nCertainly, you win, otherwise you are invincible in all three worlds by anyone. Meaning: The word 'udapasy' clearly indicates that one should not make even a slight attempt to understand the ultimate truth through the method of mental contemplation (gyan), because it can only lead to incomplete and unspecialized knowledge of God. The word 'Jivanti' indicates that the devotee who always listens to Krishna,\ngoes to the abode of God even if he does nothing and only listens to stories about God,\nremains. Srila Svanatan Goswami has explained the words 'Tanuvaangmanobhih' in three ways. In the context of devotees, that they are able to win over Lord Krishna through their mind, speech, and actions. In this way, in Krishna consciousness,\nBeing complete, they can touch the lotus feet of those with their hands, call them with their voice,\nand by contemplating them, they can have a direct vision of them within their mind.\nIn the context of devotees, the word 'Tanuvaangmanobhih' denotes victory, and it indicates that those who\ndo not have love and devotion to Krishna cannot conquer the ultimate truth with their physical strength, eloquence, or mental power. Despite all efforts, the ultimate truth remains beyond their reach. In the context of the word 'Jitah', the word 'Tanuvaangmanobhih' indicates that pure devotees conquer the body, mind, and speech of Lord Krishna. Lord Krishna, because of being with his pure devotees, is won by the body.\nAnd by constantly praising his devotees, by speech, and by constantly contemplating his beloved devotees, he is won by the mind. Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti Thakur, while explaining the words 'Tanuvaangmanobhih', has associated it with 'Namanthanah'.\nHe has done so. They explain that devotees, while respecting the stories about God through mind, speech, and action,\ncan take full advantage of them.\nWhile offering prayers to the stories of God, the devotee should ensure that\nhe touches the ground with his hands and head, and keeps his body engaged in service. He, Shreebhadbhagavat and\nBhagavad Gita like divine texts and in praising the devotees who propagate these texts,\nuses his speech\nand while listening to the stories of God, experiences respect and joy\nuses his mind. In this way, the devotee who has attained even a little knowledge about Krishna"
